+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: chalk (chalk-ir)
+
+14 hegel property tests were added in `chalk-ir/tests/hegel_properties.rs`,
+covering ~12 distinct properties of chalk-ir's core term algebra: folding,
+de Bruijn index shifting, and substitution. `hegeltest` 0.28.2 and
+`chalk-integration` (path, for the `ChalkIr` interner — the same interner
+every other test in the workspace uses; this is a cargo-supported cyclic
+dev-dependency, which is why the tests live in `tests/` rather than a
+`#[cfg(test)]` module) were added as dev-dependencies of `chalk-ir`.
+chalk-ir previously had almost no tests of its own (one internal module in
+`fold/in_place.rs`).
+
+Terms are generated by a recursive composite generator over
+`Ty`/`Lifetime`/`Const`/`GenericArg` covering 15 `TyKind` variants,
+including `Function` (which introduces a binder level) and free bound
+variables at configurable de Bruijn depths. A `TermCfg` switch restricts
+generation to the "all free variables at the innermost binder, type-kind
+only" domain that `SubstFolder` requires, or to fully closed terms.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+1. **Identity fold is the identity** (`identity_fold_is_identity`).
+   Folding with a `FallibleTypeFolder` that overrides nothing must
+   reconstruct the term exactly. Evidence: the fold.rs docs ("will
+   reconstruct itself"); every `TyKind` arm of `try_super_fold_with`
+   destructures and re-interns, so a dropped/reordered field fails this.
+
+2. **Shift-in then shift-out round-trips**
+   (`shift_in_then_out_is_identity`): for any term and any binder depth
+   `n <= 3`, `shifted_in_from(n)` then `shifted_out_to(n)` returns `Ok`
+   of the original. Evidence: `Shift` trait docs describe these as
+   inverse motions across binders.
+
+3. **Shift-out then shift-in round-trips when defined**
+   (`shift_out_then_in_is_identity`): if `shifted_out` succeeds (no
+   variable was bound by the eliminated level), `shifted_in` restores the
+   original term.
+
+4. **`needs_shift` agrees with shifting**
+   (`needs_shift_iff_shift_changes_term`): `ty.needs_shift()` (a
+   *visitor*-based free-variable search via `has_free_vars`) holds iff
+   `shifted_in` (a *folder*) actually changes the term. Cross-checks the
+   visit and fold implementations of "free variable" against each other.
+
+5. **`BoundVar` shift algebra round-trips** (`bound_var_shift_roundtrip`,
+   pure, no interner): `shifted_in_from(o)` then `shifted_out_to(o)` is
+   `Some(original)` for depths up to 2^16 (bounded because of suspected
+   bug #1 below).
+
+6. **`shifted_out_to` is `None` exactly when `within`**
+   (`shifted_out_none_iff_within`, full `u32` range). Evidence: doc on
+   `DebruijnIndex::shifted_out_to` ("will return None if the self index
+   is internal to the outer binder").
+
+7. **The two independent substitution folders agree**
+   (`subst_and_substitution_apply_agree`). chalk-ir has two separate
+   substitution implementations: `Subst` (fold/subst.rs, used by
+   `Binders::substitute`) and `SubstFolder` (lib.rs, used by
+   `Substitution::apply`). On terms in both domains (all free variables
+   type-kind at the innermost binder, indices < n, closed parameters)
+   they must produce identical results.
+
+8. **Substituting a shifted-in term is a no-op**
+   (`subst_after_shift_in_is_identity`): `Subst::apply(params,
+   ty.shifted_in())` returns `ty` for *any* params (even empty), because
+   the shift guarantees no variable refers to the eliminated binder and
+   the substitution's binder-elimination shifts the rest back down.
+   Evidence: the worked `for<A,B>` example in `Subst`'s docs.
+
+9. **`identity_substitution` is recognized by `is_identity_subst`**
+   (`identity_substitution_is_recognized`). Evidence: the "?0 := ?0, ?1
+   := ?1" characterization on `is_identity_subst`.
+
+10. **Substituting the identity substitution is a no-op**
+    (`substitute_identity_is_noop`) on terms with free innermost
+    variables under a matching `Binders`.
+
+11. **`could_match` is reflexive** (`could_match_is_reflexive`), with a
+    stub `UnificationDatabase` returning invariant variances. Evidence:
+    doc "a fast check to see whether two things could ever possibly
+    match" — an over-approximation of unifiability must at minimum accept
+    a term against itself; a missing arm in its big `TyKind` match breaks
+    this.
+
+12. **`Ty` -> `GenericArg` cast round-trips**
+    (`cast_ty_to_generic_arg_roundtrips`): `arg.ty()` returns the
+    original type.
+
+Plus the two deterministically pinned KNOWN FAILURE tests described under
+Suspected bugs.
+
+## Test results
+
+`cargo test -p chalk-ir --test hegel_properties`: **12 passed, 2 failed
+(both intentional KNOWN FAILURE pins, deterministic — every generated
+case fails)**. Results are identical at the default 100 cases and at an
+exploratory 10x run (`test_cases = 1000`). Liveness was verified by
+temporarily inverting the assertion in `shift_in_then_out_is_identity`
+and observing a shrunk counterexample (`Str`, `n = 0`), then reverting.
+Pre-existing `chalk-ir --lib` tests still pass (6/6). `.hegel/` was added
+to `.gitignore`.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+1. **`DebruijnIndex::shifted_in_from` overflows on large depths**
+   (pinned by `debruijn_shift_overflows_at_boundary`).
+   `chalk-ir/src/lib.rs` computes `self.depth() + outer_binder.depth()`
+   with unchecked arithmetic: for sums above `u32::MAX` this panics with
+   "attempt to add with overflow" in debug builds and silently wraps —
+   corrupting the index, breaking the shift-in/shift-out inverse law —
+   in release builds. Neither `DebruijnIndex::new` nor `shifted_in_from`
+   documents any bound on depth. `BoundVar::shifted_in_from` inherits
+   the problem. Severity: low in practice (depths are binder-nesting
+   counts), but it is an undocumented panic/silent-wrap on a public API.
+
+2. **`Subst` doc/impl contradiction on out-of-range variables** (pinned
+   by `subst_leaves_out_of_range_vars_untouched`). The doc comment on
+   `Subst::parameters` (chalk-ir/src/fold/subst.rs) says: "A reference
+   to a free variable with index `i` will be mapped to `parameters[i]`
+   -- if `i > parameters.len()`, then we will leave the variable
+   untouched." The implementation (`fold_free_var_ty` and siblings)
+   indexes `self.parameters[index]` unconditionally and panics with an
+   index-out-of-bounds error instead. Either the docs or the code is
+   wrong. (`Binders::substitute` independently asserts length equality,
+   so the documented "untouched" path is unreachable through that entry
+   point — evidence the doc, not the panic, is what's stale, but the
+   public `Subst::apply` contract as written is violated.)
+
+3. **Observation (not pinned, generator constrained instead): fold/visit
+   asymmetry on the type of a `Const`.** `Const::try_super_fold_with`
+   folds the const's `ty` field (for `Concrete` values, and the default
+   `try_fold_free_var_const` folds it too), but `Const::super_visit_with`
+   never visits the `ty` field at all. A free `BoundVar` inside a
+   const's type is therefore *shifted by the fold-based shifter but
+   invisible to the visitor-based `needs_shift`/`has_free_vars`*, which
+   initially falsified property 4 (counterexample: `[Str; 0]` whose
+   length const had type `^0.0`). chalk treats "const types don't have
+   free variables" as an IR invariant (comment in
+   `Shifter::fold_free_var_const`), so such terms are outside the
+   intended domain and the const-type generator now only produces closed
+   types (documented in `arb_const`). Still worth an upstream look: the
+   invariant is enforced nowhere, and fold and visit disagree about it.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **`needs_shift` iff shift changes term, over unrestricted consts** —
+  the original form of property 4 generated bound variables inside const
+  *types* and failed; per the analysis above this is a domain violation
+  of an informal IR invariant rather than a clear-cut bug, so the
+  generator was constrained rather than the failure pinned (see
+  Suspected bugs #3).
+- **`Substitution::apply` vs `Subst::apply` over arbitrary free
+  variables** — not unsound but deliberately narrowed: `SubstFolder`
+  *asserts* every free variable lives at the innermost binder, so the
+  equivalence property is only stated on that shared domain
+  (`TermCfg::innermost_ty_only`).
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The recursive-generator pattern from the Rust reference (`compose!` +
+  `BoxedGenerator<'static, T>` with a depth budget) worked unchanged for
+  a five-way mutually recursive IR (`Ty`/`Lifetime`/`Const`/
+  `GenericArg`/substitutions); passing a small `Copy` config struct
+  through the recursion made one generator serve three different input
+  domains (arbitrary / innermost-only / closed).
+- Interner-parameterized crates (chalk-ir is generic over `I: Interner`
+  with no concrete interner in-crate) need the workspace's test interner
+  crate as a *cyclic path dev-dependency*, and the tests must go in
+  `tests/` (integration), not `#[cfg(test)]` modules — unit tests get a
+  second, type-incompatible build of the crate under test. Worth a note
+  in the Rust reference's Setup section.
+- The shared scratchpad directory collides across concurrent agent
+  sessions on generic filenames (`run1.log`); a run log for this session
+  was briefly clobbered by another session's brotli output. Use unique
+  subdirectories.
